1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,762
A broken zipper doesn't mean a ruined
jacket.

2
00:00:03,216 --> 00:00:05,238
Before you throw it away or pay for

3
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:07,372
a repair, try this.

4
00:00:08,336 --> 00:00:11,008
Seven quick fixes for a zipper that is

5
00:00:11,018 --> 00:00:14,198
stuck, split, or off its track.

6
00:00:15,349 --> 00:00:18,427
Stay for step four: it closes a splitting

7
00:00:18,437 --> 00:00:21,095
zipper with just a pair of pliers.

8
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:22,639
WikiTube.

9
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:25,007
Whatever it is, here's how.

10
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,282
Zippers fail in three ways: fabric gets
caught,

11
00:00:29,457 --> 00:00:32,523
the teeth stop closing behind the slider,
or

12
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:34,425
the slider comes off the track.

13
00:00:34,879 --> 00:00:37,643
Each has its own fix, so first we'll

14
00:00:37,653 --> 00:00:39,499
find out which one you have.

15
00:00:39,952 --> 00:00:42,552
Most repairs need only things from a
drawer

16
00:00:42,611 --> 00:00:43,875
or a sewing kit.

17
00:00:44,329 --> 00:00:45,838
Work gently.

18
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,920
You'll need needle-nose pliers, a graphite
pencil or

19
00:00:49,930 --> 00:00:53,704
a bar of soap, tweezers, a replacement
slider

20
00:00:53,714 --> 00:00:55,968
in the right size, and a small piece

21
00:00:55,978 --> 00:00:57,974
of cloth to protect the metal.

22
00:00:58,533 --> 00:00:59,426
Step one.

23
00:00:59,787 --> 00:01:02,526
Look closely at the zipper, with good
light.

24
00:01:03,072 --> 00:01:05,289
Is fabric caught between the teeth?

25
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:08,403
Do the teeth separate right behind the
slider

26
00:01:08,413 --> 00:01:09,771
after you pull it up?

27
00:01:10,225 --> 00:01:13,660
Or has the slider slid completely off one
side?

28
00:01:14,625 --> 00:01:17,215
Run the slider up and down slowly, and

29
00:01:17,225 --> 00:01:19,465
feel for the spot where it hesitates.

30
00:01:19,919 --> 00:01:22,928
Also check for missing or bent teeth,
because

31
00:01:22,938 --> 00:01:25,424
a zipper with several teeth missing can't
be

32
00:01:25,434 --> 00:01:26,710
fixed this way.

33
00:01:27,373 --> 00:01:30,010
Knowing the problem first saves you from
forcing

34
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,332
it, which is the fastest way to break

35
00:01:32,342 --> 00:01:33,850
a zipper for good.

36
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,732
Take a photo of the damage if you

37
00:01:36,742 --> 00:01:39,565
want to compare it before and after, or

38
00:01:39,575 --> 00:01:40,944
to show the shop.

39
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:42,521
Step two.

40
00:01:43,184 --> 00:01:47,513
If fabric or thread is caught, stop
pulling right away.

41
00:01:48,315 --> 00:01:50,697
Move the slider back down a few
millimetres

42
00:01:50,707 --> 00:01:53,251
to loosen the fabric, then gently pull the

43
00:01:53,261 --> 00:01:56,397
material away from the teeth with tweezers
or

44
00:01:56,407 --> 00:01:57,776
the point of a pin.

45
00:01:58,741 --> 00:02:02,049
Work slowly and do a little at a time.

46
00:02:02,595 --> 00:02:05,032
If the fabric is stuck on both sides,

47
00:02:05,057 --> 00:02:07,752
hold the zipper flat and wiggle the slider

48
00:02:07,762 --> 00:02:10,373
back and forth in tiny movements.

49
00:02:11,338 --> 00:02:14,231
A stubborn snag can sometimes be lifted
with

50
00:02:14,241 --> 00:02:15,923
a small sewing needle.

51
00:02:16,586 --> 00:02:19,708
Once it is free, check the cloth for
damage.

52
00:02:20,162 --> 00:02:22,416
If a few threads are pulled, trim them

53
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:25,466
with small scissors, so they can't catch
again.

54
00:02:26,431 --> 00:02:28,775
Then test the zipper slowly.

55
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:32,040
If it moves but feels stiff, the next

56
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:33,756
step is for you.

57
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:35,321
Step three.

58
00:02:35,775 --> 00:02:38,374
If the zipper is stiff but not caught,

59
00:02:38,503 --> 00:02:41,254
dirt or friction is the usual cause.

60
00:02:42,219 --> 00:02:44,914
Rub a graphite pencil, a dry bar of

61
00:02:44,924 --> 00:02:48,072
soap or a candle stub along both rows

62
00:02:48,082 --> 00:02:50,823
of teeth, then move the slider up and

63
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:53,177
down several times to spread it.

64
00:02:54,142 --> 00:02:57,554
On light fabric, use soap or candle wax,

65
00:02:57,579 --> 00:03:00,248
because graphite can leave grey marks.

66
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:03,583
For a very dirty zipper, brush the teeth

67
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:05,951
first with an old toothbrush and a little

68
00:03:05,961 --> 00:03:08,073
water, and let it dry.

69
00:03:08,736 --> 00:03:11,464
You will feel the improvement straight
away.

70
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:14,310
This is also the best fix for zippers

71
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,875
on boots, bags and tents, which collect
dust

72
00:03:17,885 --> 00:03:19,695
and grit all the time.

73
00:03:20,358 --> 00:03:22,238
Repeat once a year.

74
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:23,856
Step four.

75
00:03:24,403 --> 00:03:26,817
When the teeth open up behind the slider,

76
00:03:26,841 --> 00:03:30,532
the slider has usually stretched and no
longer grips.

77
00:03:30,986 --> 00:03:32,900
Move it to the bottom of the zipper,

78
00:03:33,261 --> 00:03:36,279
and place a cloth over it to protect the
metal.

79
00:03:36,825 --> 00:03:39,869
Using pliers, gently squeeze the slider on
its

80
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:43,131
two sides, just a little, and test it

81
00:03:43,141 --> 00:03:44,255
by zipping up.

82
00:03:45,405 --> 00:03:49,375
Repeat with tiny squeezes until the zipper
closes firmly.

83
00:03:49,736 --> 00:03:53,601
Too much force can crush the slider, so go
slowly.

84
00:03:54,751 --> 00:03:56,956
If you can see the slider is cracked,

85
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:58,918
skip ahead and replace it.

86
00:03:59,465 --> 00:04:01,730
After the fix, zip the zipper up and

87
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:05,130
down at least ten times to confirm that it
holds.

88
00:04:05,676 --> 00:04:08,685
If it still opens, one more tiny squeeze

89
00:04:08,695 --> 00:04:10,192
will usually do it.

90
00:04:10,854 --> 00:04:13,607
If the slider has come off completely,
that's

91
00:04:13,617 --> 00:04:14,870
the next fix.

92
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:16,512
Step five.

93
00:04:16,873 --> 00:04:19,322
If the slider has come off one side,

94
00:04:19,381 --> 00:04:22,622
first remove the bottom stop, the little
metal

95
00:04:22,632 --> 00:04:25,533
piece at the end, using pliers.

96
00:04:26,196 --> 00:04:28,926
Then line up both rows of teeth, and

97
00:04:28,936 --> 00:04:31,689
slide the slider on from the bottom, one

98
00:04:31,699 --> 00:04:35,170
side at a time, so the teeth enter evenly.

99
00:04:35,716 --> 00:04:37,889
Zip it up a few centimetres to check

100
00:04:37,899 --> 00:04:39,547
that it closes correctly.

101
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,820
If the teeth cross over, start again.

102
00:04:43,622 --> 00:04:46,921
Finally, fit the stop back in place, or

103
00:04:46,931 --> 00:04:49,464
sew a few firm stitches across the tape

104
00:04:49,474 --> 00:04:50,947
to make a new stop.

105
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:54,282
If the zipper is plastic, the same method

106
00:04:54,292 --> 00:04:58,229
works, but be gentle, because plastic
teeth are

107
00:04:58,239 --> 00:05:00,212
easier to break than metal.

108
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:01,904
Step six.

109
00:05:03,054 --> 00:05:05,921
Sometimes the slider is just worn out.

110
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:08,579
Look for a number stamped on the back,

111
00:05:08,813 --> 00:05:12,146
like 3, 5 or 8, which gives the

112
00:05:12,156 --> 00:05:15,176
zipper size, and buy a matching slider at

113
00:05:15,186 --> 00:05:17,031
a craft or sewing shop.

114
00:05:17,996 --> 00:05:20,654
Remove the old one and the bottom stop,

115
00:05:20,713 --> 00:05:23,431
as in the previous step, and slide the

116
00:05:23,441 --> 00:05:24,439
new one on.

117
00:05:24,985 --> 00:05:27,042
You can also remove the top stop to

118
00:05:27,052 --> 00:05:28,816
do the job from the other end.

119
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:32,833
Then add a new stop, either with pliers

120
00:05:32,857 --> 00:05:34,400
or with strong thread.

121
00:05:34,947 --> 00:05:37,918
A new slider usually costs only a few
dollars.

122
00:05:38,279 --> 00:05:41,497
Repair shops often charge much more, so
doing

123
00:05:41,507 --> 00:05:43,433
it yourself is worth a try.

124
00:05:44,096 --> 00:05:48,472
One step is left: keeping your zippers out
of trouble.

125
00:05:49,033 --> 00:05:50,251
Step seven.

126
00:05:51,053 --> 00:05:54,384
A few habits will keep your zippers
working for years.

127
00:05:55,349 --> 00:05:58,264
Close them fully before washing, so the
teeth

128
00:05:58,274 --> 00:06:01,060
don't bend or snag other clothes.

129
00:06:02,210 --> 00:06:05,462
Don't overstuff bags or jackets, as the
strain

130
00:06:05,472 --> 00:06:07,352
makes the teeth separate.

131
00:06:08,503 --> 00:06:11,012
Pull the slider by the tab, in line

132
00:06:11,022 --> 00:06:13,703
with the zipper, and not at an angle.

133
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,724
Once or twice a year, wipe the teeth

134
00:06:16,734 --> 00:06:19,264
and rub them with soap or wax.

135
00:06:19,625 --> 00:06:22,123
If a zipper starts to feel rough, deal

136
00:06:22,133 --> 00:06:26,230
with it right away, because small problems
get worse quickly.

137
00:06:26,893 --> 00:06:29,240
A few seconds of care now will save

138
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:31,025
you a repair later.

139
00:06:31,567 --> 00:06:33,856
If none of the fixes worked, look at

140
00:06:33,866 --> 00:06:34,910
the teeth again.

141
00:06:35,712 --> 00:06:39,705
Squeezing the slider cannot repair missing
or cracked teeth.

142
00:06:40,507 --> 00:06:43,342
A bent metal tooth can sometimes be
straightened

143
00:06:43,352 --> 00:06:44,779
with small pliers.

144
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:47,127
If the teeth are damaged and the slider

145
00:06:47,137 --> 00:06:49,925
is fine, replacing the whole zipper is the

146
00:06:49,935 --> 00:06:51,640
only lasting fix.

147
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:55,659
Some jobs belong to a tailor or a repair
shop.

148
00:06:56,113 --> 00:06:58,111
Replacing a full zipper on a coat or

149
00:06:58,121 --> 00:07:01,825
jeans means unpicking seams and sewing
them back,

150
00:07:02,371 --> 00:07:04,611
which is hard to do neatly at home.

151
00:07:05,065 --> 00:07:09,592
Leather, down jackets and expensive pieces
deserve a professional.

152
00:07:10,394 --> 00:07:13,263
Ask for a price first, because a repair

153
00:07:13,273 --> 00:07:16,035
often costs far less than a new coat.

154
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:18,483
Keep a few spare sliders in your sewing

155
00:07:18,493 --> 00:07:21,023
kit, because they are small and cheap.

156
00:07:21,383 --> 00:07:24,543
Fix small problems at once, since a zipper

157
00:07:24,553 --> 00:07:27,431
that keeps splitting wears the slider down
further.

158
00:07:28,395 --> 00:07:30,080
Zip a jacket to the top when you

159
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:33,886
hang it, and take hard objects out of bag
pockets.

160
00:07:34,433 --> 00:07:36,534
Here are the mistakes to avoid.

161
00:07:37,684 --> 00:07:40,527
First, never force a stuck zipper.

162
00:07:40,888 --> 00:07:43,479
Pulling harder bends the teeth and turns
an

163
00:07:43,489 --> 00:07:45,927
easy fix into a permanent break.

164
00:07:47,077 --> 00:07:50,071
Second, don't use oil or grease on
clothing,

165
00:07:50,130 --> 00:07:51,815
as it can leave marks that are very

166
00:07:51,825 --> 00:07:53,009
hard to remove.

167
00:07:53,369 --> 00:07:56,480
Use soap, wax or graphite instead.

168
00:07:57,143 --> 00:08:01,019
And third, don't squeeze a slider too hard
with pliers.

169
00:08:02,170 --> 00:08:04,865
A small amount of pressure works, while
too

170
00:08:04,875 --> 00:08:06,546
much can crack the metal.

171
00:08:07,696 --> 00:08:11,503
Patience is the real secret to every
zipper repair.

172
00:08:12,067 --> 00:08:13,633
Let's run through it again.

173
00:08:13,924 --> 00:08:15,607
Find out what is wrong.

174
00:08:16,757 --> 00:08:18,707
Free any caught fabric.

175
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:20,889
Lubricate a stiff zipper.

176
00:08:21,854 --> 00:08:23,757
Close a zipper that splits.

177
00:08:24,118 --> 00:08:25,974
Put a slider back on track.

178
00:08:27,125 --> 00:08:29,028
Replace a worn-out slider.

179
00:08:29,481 --> 00:08:32,650
And keep your zippers healthy with a few
simple habits.

180
00:08:33,104 --> 00:08:35,938
If it still fails, the bonus sections
cover

181
00:08:35,948 --> 00:08:37,851
a tailor and prevention.

182
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,872
That's how you fix a zipper at home.

183
00:08:41,326 --> 00:08:44,866
If this saved your jacket, subscribe to
WikiTube,

184
00:08:44,925 --> 00:08:48,337
because we publish a new how-to every
single day.

185
00:08:48,884 --> 00:08:51,021
Tell us in the comments which zipper
problem

186
00:08:51,031 --> 00:08:53,111
you had, and what you would like us

187
00:08:53,121 --> 00:08:54,490
to explain next.

188
00:08:55,037 --> 00:08:56,336
See you tomorrow.

189
00:08:56,433 --> 00:08:57,628
That's WikiTube.

190
00:08:57,919 --> 00:08:59,927
Whatever it is, here's how.
